WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.160
Linux separates normal users from administrative authority.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:07.060
A normal user can run applications, create personal files,

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.760
and perform work allowed by that account.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.980
The root account is different.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:17.880
Root can modify system configuration, install packages, change ownership, stop services,

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.220
read many protected files, and affect other users.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.160
That makes root powerful, but also high impact.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.140
Mistakes made with root privileges can break systems, expose data,

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.680
or remove evidence needed for troubleshooting.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.460
Sudo is a controlled way to grant administrative capability without having everyone sign

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.120
in directly as root.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:42.300
In a well-managed system, sudo access is intentional: who has it, why they have it,

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.080
what they are allowed to do, and how that

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.060
access is reviewed.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.720
Broad sudo access may be convenient, but it creates risk.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.340
It can also hide accountability if shared administrator accounts are used instead of

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.000
unique user accounts.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.300
Groups help organize access.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.080
A group may control access to files, devices, application directories,

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.580
operational functions, or administrative capabilities.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.320
Group membership should be treated like permission, not decoration.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:15.780
Privileged groups deserve periodic review, especially after role changes,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
contractor offboarding, migrations, or emergency access exceptions.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.220
Service accounts also need care.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.880
A service account should have an owner, purpose, authentication method, and lifecycle.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.780
It should not be a forgotten account with broad access and no review.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.360
Good Linux security starts with identity hygiene: unique accounts, limited privilege,

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:43.340
strong authentication, documented exceptions, and regular validation that users, groups,

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.640
sudo rights, and

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.500
service accounts still match the system's purpose.
